course: dictionary
lesson: 6453
title: Nappy or diaper? Same thing, different names
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words mean the same thing but are used in different places. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, people say nappy. In the USA and Canada, people say diaper for the same thing.
explain: Context helps you work out what a word means. «In Galway, Nuala changes her baby brother's nappy while Mam's in the shower.» Changing a baby and doing it while Mam is busy tells you that a nappy is something a baby wears.
explain: Look at the other clues too. «Babies wear nappies, which are changed six to ten times a day, and nappy cream stops sore skin.» Worn by babies, changed often, and used with cream for sore skin: a nappy is a pad that babies wear.
fact: A nappy is a thick, soft pad that babies wear around their bottom.
fact: A nappy catches a baby's wee and poo.
fact: «Nappy cream stops sore skin.» It helps when a baby's skin gets sore.
fact: Disposable nappies make a lot of rubbish, so some families use washable cloth nappies.
fact: In the USA and Canada, people say diaper instead of nappy.
exercise: What is a nappy? | *A thick, soft pad worn by babies around their bottom | A bag for carrying toys | A pair of trousers worn by grown-ups | A cloth for wiping tables
exercise: Which word do people in the USA and Canada use for a nappy? | Bib | Blanket | *Diaper | Sock
exercise: «Disposable nappies make a lot of rubbish.» Why do some families choose washable cloth nappies? | To keep the baby warm in the sun | To make the baby taller | *To make less rubbish | To stop the baby from crying
test: «Babies wear nappies, which are changed six to ten times a day.» How often are nappies changed? | Once a week | *Six to ten times a day | Once a year | Never
test: «Nappy cream stops sore skin.» What does nappy cream do? | *Helps stop sore skin | Keeps the baby cold | Makes the baby sleepy | Washes the nappy
exam: «The changing bag by the door is stuffed with nappies.» What does stuffed suggest? | Empty | Lost | *Packed full | Wet through
